{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J2 — Extract the Jira-family shared layer out of the Cloud adapter\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`adapters/jira/` currently owns units that are Jira-family-general, not Cloud-specific: the local↔Jira value\nmaps and sanitizers (`jira_fields.py`), the `rebar-id` identity convention (`identity.py`), the link relation\nvocabulary (`jira_fields._RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK`), and the absence-probe classifier (`probe.py`). PR #120\nconsumed these from a second adapter as **private** cross-package imports, which ADR 0035 never sanctioned —\nits seam is one level deep, core ⇄ adapter, with `adapters/jira/` as one backend's concrete implementation.\n\n## Approach\n\nCreate `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/` and move the **non-location-pinned** units\ninto it with **public** names, then re-point the Cloud adapter's imports. Behavior is unchanged and proven so by\nthe characterization oracles pinned in J1 [rebar:acb2-1823-2f0d-415b], which must pass untouched across this\nchange.\n\n**The move is by SYMBOL, not by file** — and only symbols out of the three modules that carry no by-path load\nreferences. Verified at plan time with the ADR-prescribed grep\n(`lazy_load(|_load_sibling_keyed(|spec_from_file_location(`) plus a filename-string sweep over `src/` and\n`tests/`: `jira_fields.py`, `identity.py`, and `probe.py` appear in **no** by-path load site.\n\n- `identity.py` → relocates whole (`JiraIdentityConvention`). Imports nothing from `adapters/jira/`.\n- `probe.py` → `classify_probe_response` + `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES` move. Its live `probe()` / `_resolve_env()`\n  stay in `adapters/jira/`: they read Cloud's ACLI settings, which is Cloud-specific transport.\n- `jira_fields.py` → **splits.** It cannot relocate wholesale, because it imports from two pinned modules:\n  `fit_text_to_adf_limit` from `adapters/jira/adf.py` (used by `_sanitize_description`) and\n  `truncate_comment_body` / `_JIRA_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS` from `adapters/jira/comment_limits.py` (used by\n  `_sanitize_comment`). Moving the file would make `jira_family/` import `adapters/jira/`, inverting the\n  dependency direction this story's own structural oracle forbids. So:\n  - **Move to `jira_family/` outright:** `_sanitize_label`, `_sanitize_summary`, `InvalidLabelError`,\n    `_RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK`, and the value-map literals (see de-duplication below). None touch ADF or comment\n    limits — `_sanitize_summary` is a pure character truncation against `_JIRA_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS`.\n  - **Move in contract-parameterized form:** `_sanitize_description` and `_sanitize_comment`. Their vendor\n    dependency is injected rather than imported — `_sanitize_description` receives the `RichTextCodec` (its\n    `fit_text_to_adf_limit` call *is* the rich-text seam that J3 [rebar:aac8-b283-22c1-4e66] formalizes), and\n    comment truncation receives the vendor comment-limit contract. Cloud builds both objects in\n    `adapters/jira/` from its pinned `adf.py` / `comment_limits.py`; DC builds wiki-markup/plain-text\n    equivalents in J6/J7. Observable behavior for Cloud is unchanged and pinned by J1's oracles.\n  - `adapters/jira/jira_fields.py` therefore **remains as a thin Cloud-side module** that constructs the Cloud\n    contract objects and re-exports nothing — its ACLI-facing callers (`acli.py`, `acli_cli_ops.py`,\n    `acli_graph.py`) are re-pointed at the `jira_family` symbols directly, so no shim remains.\n\n  If J3's `RichTextCodec` shape is not yet settled when this story is implemented, inject a minimal\n  `Callable[[str], str]` fit function and let J3 widen it to the full contract — do NOT import the pinned\n  module from `jira_family/` as a stopgap.\n\n**`outbound_fields.py`, `adf.py`, and `comment_limits.py` do NOT move** — they are location-pinned per ADR 0035\n§(a), and the pin is live, not merely historical:\n\n- `adapters/jira/adf.py` is loaded by path from `inbound_fields.py:36` and\n  `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:69` (`lazy_load(_ADF_KEY, \"adapters/jira/adf.py\")`), plus ~13 test files.\n- `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py` is loaded by path from `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_config.py:193`,\n  `tests/integration/rebar_reconciler/test_reconcile_roundtrip.py:72`, and\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_commits_on_ticket.py:121`.\n- `adapters/jira/comment_limits.py` is loaded by path from ~9 test files.\n\nSo the units inside them — `_map_local_to_jira_fields`, `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS`, `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY`, and\nthe ADF encoder — are shared **by dependency inversion, not relocation**: the pinned module imports the\n`jira_family` contracts (never the reverse) and is parameterized by them, so DC reuses one implementation with\nno file moving and `_loader.py` untouched. Relocating them is ADR 0035 Phase 2's work and is out of scope; if\nthis story appears to require *moving* a pinned file, stop and record the blocker rather than moving it.\n\n**Pinned means \"cannot change path\", not \"cannot be edited\"** — replacing a dict literal inside\n`outbound_fields.py` with an import from `jira_family/` is safe, because the loader resolves the file by path\nand does not care about its contents.\n\n### De-duplicating the value maps (the pre-existing violation this story must not preserve)\n\nThe local→Jira status/priority maps exist TODAY in two independently-referenced copies that have already\ndrifted:\n\n- `jira_fields.py`'s `_LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` / `_LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA` — read **only** by the ACLI transport\n  (`acli.py:69-70,143,171`; `acli_cli_ops.py:34,343,354`).\n- the pinned `outbound_fields.py`'s `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS` / `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY` — read by the `Backend`\n  port through `backend.py`'s `map_fields_to_remote`.\n- The drift: the `outbound_fields` status map has a `\"deleted\": \"Done\"` entry the `jira_fields` copy lacks.\n\nMoving only the `jira_fields` copy would leave Cloud's backend reading a second copy, so this story makes\n`adapters/jira_family/` the **sole definition site**: both existing dict literals are deleted and replaced by\nimports from `jira_family/`, keeping each caller's current observable behavior. Because the ACLI path currently\nhas no entry for `deleted` and reaches its `status.replace(\"_\", \" \").title()` fallback, preserving behavior\nmeans the unified map is the `outbound_fields` superset AND the ACLI call site keeps its existing fallback\nsemantics for keys it did not previously map — no caller's output changes in this story.\n\nDeciding which `deleted` behavior is *correct* is deliberately **out of scope** here (it is a behavior change,\nnot a move): bug `fe15-3bc4-ed70-4b61` (`controlled-ornate-honeybee`) owns that decision and the missing\nACLI-side characterization pin.\n\nPer ADR 0035 decision 4, **no re-export shims** are left at the old paths: tests patch module-qualified, so a\nshim would create a patch-binding bug. Every importer and every `mock.patch` string is updated in this same\nchange, and each moved module keeps exactly one canonical path.\n\n`adapters/jira/`'s ACLI transport (`acli*.py`) is explicitly **not** moved and not modified: it is the\nCloud-specific, live-validated part.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** promoting these into the backend-neutral core. They are Jira-specific — a GitHub\nbackend would want none of them — so the neutral core is the wrong home and would re-create the coupling ADR\n0035's Phase 2 removed.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** leaving them in `adapters/jira/` and importing across adapters (PR #120's approach).\nIt makes a concrete backend double as a shared library with no contract, and points a dependency edge sideways\nbetween siblings.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/` (new package).\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/` — `identity.py` relocated; `probe.py`'s pure classifier\n  relocated (live probe stays); `jira_fields.py` split by symbol (file stays as the Cloud-side contract\n  construction site); `backend.py` and `outbound_fields.py` imports updated; `acli*.py`, `adf.py`,\n  `comment_limits.py` untouched in location.\n- Every internal importer and every test `mock.patch` string naming the old paths.\n- **Out of scope:** moving any location-pinned module, and any edit to `_loader.py`.\n\n## Testing\n\nContract tier plus the pinned characterization oracles: the mechanism is a relocation, so the oracle is that\nobservable behavior is identical and that no import or patch target dangles.\n\n- happy path: the full existing suite passes, including J1's exact-value characterization assertions, with no\n  edits to those assertions.\n- structural oracle: a test asserts no module under `adapters/jira/` imports a name from a sibling adapter\n  package, and that `adapters/jira_family/` imports from neither `adapters/jira/` nor\n  `adapters/jira_datacenter/` — pinning the acyclic direction this epic exists to establish.\n- negative control: `grep` confirms no re-export shim module remains at any old path.\n- collateral invariant: the reconciler's dynamic loader still resolves every by-path module (the existing loader\n  tests pass unchanged), and a test asserts each pinned filename (`adapters/jira/adf.py`,\n  `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`, `adapters/jira/comment_limits.py`) still exists at its pinned path —\n  proving no location-pinned file moved.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `make test` passes with J1's characterization assertions unmodified\n- [ ] no module under `adapters/jira/` imports a name from a sibling adapter package, and no module under\n      `adapters/jira_family/` imports from `adapters/jira/` or `adapters/jira_datacenter/` — verified by grep at\n      this story's merge time (the reciprocal check on `adapters/jira_datacenter/` is J7's AC, since that\n      package does not exist yet)\n- [ ] no re-export shim exists at any pre-move path (verified by `grep` for the old module names)\n- [ ] every `mock.patch(` string in `tests/` naming a moved module resolves to the new path (`make test` green\n      is the check, since a stale patch target raises)\n- [ ] `adapters/jira/acli*.py` are byte-identical to their pre-change state (`git diff --stat` shows no change)\n- [ ] `adapters/jira/adf.py`, `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`, and `adapters/jira/comment_limits.py` still\n      exist at those exact paths, and `_loader.py` is unchanged (`git diff --stat` shows neither a rename nor an\n      edit to `_loader.py`)\n- [ ] no module under `adapters/jira_family/` imports from `adapters/jira/` — specifically, `jira_family/`\n      contains no import of `adf`, `comment_limits`, `outbound_fields`, or any `acli*` module; the\n      ADF/comment-limit dependencies of `_sanitize_description` / `_sanitize_comment` arrive as injected\n      contract objects (verified by grep over `adapters/jira_family/`)\n- [ ] exactly one dict literal defines the local→Jira status map and one the priority map across the engine:\n      `grep -rn` for the map literals finds them only under `adapters/jira_family/`, and both the ACLI\n      transport and `backend.py`'s `map_fields_to_remote` resolve to that single definition\n- [ ] observable behavior is unchanged for both callers despite the map unification: J1's backend\n      characterization pins pass unmodified, and the ACLI path's output for every status/priority it previously\n      produced — including the `deleted` fallback — is unchanged (pinned by a test in this story)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785290879979444001,"uuid":"5a581950-db98-4fc1-9bca-11e96ce85dfa"}